From: Ziy engine fork <ziy@ziy.app>
Date: 2026-10-15
Subject: [PATCH] dns: honour network.dns.disablePrefetchFromHTTPS, which the
 engine registers and never reads

Ziy locks network.dns.disablePrefetchFromHTTPS in the shell. It does nothing,
and the reason is the 0001 pattern in a new area: the engine documents the
capability, and no engine code reads it.

The pref is registered at modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml:15433-15437
as a bool StaticPref (mirror: always). The only occurrence of its generated
accessor anywhere in the tree is the comment at netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.cpp:
36-37, which asserts the opposite of what is true -- "network.dns.
disablePrefetchFromHTTPS is a StaticPref and needs no callback". A pref that
needs no callback still needs a consumer: DNSServiceBase::AddPrefObserver()
(that same file, :33-44) correctly declines to observe it BECAUSE it believes a
StaticPref read exists somewhere. Nothing reads it. mDisablePrefetch
(DNSServiceBase.h:31) is maintained for the sibling pref network.dns.
disablePrefetch (read at :65-68) and is applied at nsDNSService2.cpp:1039; there
is no equivalent for the HTTPS variant.

This adds the consumer, at the engine's own chosen place. nsDNSService::
AsyncResolveInternal() (nsDNSService2.cpp:1025-1041) is the single funnel every
async DNS lookup passes through, and it already returns early inside the lock
block for the pref-controlled speculative path two lines above. The new check
mirrors that shape exactly: same early return, same enclosing lock scope, same
structure, no restructuring of the surrounding code.

Why the pref matters for this product. HTTPS RR (SVCB) records are the
fingerprinting-relevant half of prefetch: an HTTPSSVC answer carries the
server's ECH config list and alt-service hints, which is per-server identity
material the querying client has no need to obtain speculatively. Ziy's shell
already refuses the whole prefetch family for the ordinary case; this closes
the one member that was silently exempt because its pref was dead.

Return NS_ERROR_NOT_AVAILABLE, not NS_ERROR_DNS_LOOKUP_QUEUE_FULL: the latter
means "queued but suppressed, retry later" and is the code the sibling pref uses
for speculative lookups. A user-disabled pref is not a queue condition, and
NS_ERROR_NOT_AVAILABLE is the engine's existing code for "this resolve cannot
be satisfied here" (it is what nsDNSService2.cpp returns for a null resolver at
the same site, :1053).

Gated behind MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING, set by the --enable-ziy-fingerprinting
configure option (patches/0002-ziy-fingerprinting-option.patch), so a build
with the gate off is stock Gecko: the pref stays registered and stays unread,
exactly as it is today.

Sources for every claim in this commit message:
  modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml:15433-15437
    network.dns.disablePrefetchFromHTTPS registration (the documentation)
  netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.cpp:33-44 AddPrefObserver (declines to observe it)
  netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.cpp:36-37 the comment that claims a reader exists
  netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.cpp:49-71 ReadPrefs (reads the sibling only)
  netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.h:31 mDisablePrefetch (no HTTPS counterpart)
  netwerk/dns/nsDNSService2.cpp:1039 the honoured sibling's early return
  netwerk/dns/nsDNSService2.cpp:1053 NS_ERROR_DNS_LOOKUP_QUEUE_FULL sibling
  netwerk/dns/nsDNSService2.cpp:11 the mozilla/StaticPrefs_network.h include
    that already carries network.dns.offline-localhost, from the same YAML
    pref group, so the new accessor needs no new include
  toolkit/components/antitracking/bouncetrackingprotection/* (pattern precedent
    for a per-BC hash keyed by BrowserId, cited in the companion report)

Not audited for consumers in files not extracted (dom/, toolkit/mozapps/): the
absence grep was run over the extracted netwerk/{cookie,dns,protocol,base},
toolkit/components/antitracking, docshell, dom/webidl, dom/storage, dom/quota,
toolkit/components/{cleardata,extensions}, browser/modules and
modules/libpref subtrees.
---
--- a/netwerk/dns/nsDNSService2.cpp
+++ b/netwerk/dns/nsDNSService2.cpp
@@ -1040,6 +1040,32 @@
       return NS_ERROR_DNS_LOOKUP_QUEUE_FULL;
     }
 
+#ifdef MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING
+    // Ziy delta 2: the HTTPS-record prefetch gate the engine registers but
+    // never reads.
+    //
+    // modules/libpref/init/StaticPrefList.yaml:15433-15437 registers
+    // network.dns.disablePrefetchFromHTTPS as a bool StaticPref, mirror: always.
+    // Nothing anywhere reads it: a grep of the whole tree for the generated
+    // accessor finds exactly one hit, and it is the comment in
+    // netwerk/dns/DNSServiceBase.cpp:36-37 asserting the opposite --
+    // "network.dns.disablePrefetchFromHTTPS is a StaticPref and needs no
+    // callback". A pref that needs no callback still needs A CONSUMER. This is
+    // the 0001 pattern: the pref layer expresses a capability, the engine
+    // documents it, and no engine code honours it.
+    //
+    // The pref's name says what it should do. Ziy locks it in the shell, and
+    // HTTPS RR (SVCB) prefetch is the fingerprinting-relevant half of prefetch:
+    // the HTTPSSVC record carries the server's ECH config and alt-service hints,
+    // which are per-server identity material a querying client does not need to
+    // see. The sibling pref network.dns.disablePrefetch IS honoured, two lines
+    // above, by exactly this early-return shape; the gate below follows it.
+    if (type == RESOLVE_TYPE_HTTPSSVC &&
+        StaticPrefs::network_dns_disablePrefetchFromHTTPS()) {
+      return NS_ERROR_NOT_AVAILABLE;
+    }
+#endif  // MOZ_ZIY_FINGERPRINTING
+
     res = mResolver;
 
     localDomain = IsLocalDomain(aHostname);
